-- drip.rofl — data lineage in observability.
--
-- A metric is renamed or deleted. Panels quietly empty and alerts stop
-- firing. That is not an error, it is an ABSENCE of data, so nobody notices
-- until the alert was needed and did not come. The reverse question is the
-- same one: before deleting a metric, nobody can say what stands on it.
--
-- WHY THIS DOMAIN. Extraction is deterministic at BOTH ends. Dashboards are
-- JSON and metric names parse out of query text; the live series list comes
-- from the metrics store's own API. Neither end needs a model or an expert,
-- so the translation layer that sinks other examples simply is not there.
-- Everything below is the OUTPUT of two dumb readers, and the file says
-- which reader wrote what.
--
-- TWO LEDGERS, and that is the point:
--   [repo]  what humans wrote: dashboards, alert rules, recording rules.
--           Written by the dashboard exporter.
--   [store] what actually exists: the series list and its label values.
--           Written by the metrics store.
-- Nobody can write into the other one's book. An exporter that asserts a
-- series exists is `forged[audit]`, mechanically (demo.ts shows it firing).
-- Conclusions live in [main] and read both ledgers through declared imports.
--
-- Data is synthetic (see README.md). 10 dashboards, 23 panels, 6 alerts,
-- 2 SLOs, 7 recording rules, 25 metric names; one metric deleted, one
-- renamed, one label VALUE gone, one query naming a metric that never
-- existed at all.

-- =========================================================================
-- 0. the ledgers
-- =========================================================================

authority(repo, dashboard_exporter).
authority(store, metrics_store).
imports(main, repo).
imports(main, store).

-- The reverse crossings, which the closed flow graph now asks about: the
-- audit rules of boot.rofl read [main], and both books reach [main], so
-- both reach [audit] in two hops. Declared deliberately -- an audit that
-- could not name the dashboard config or the metrics store could not report
-- a broken matcher in either of them.
imports(audit, repo).
imports(audit, store).

-- What this program CANNOT see. Named as facts, not as a comment, so the
-- output can print its own boundary instead of implying it covered
-- everything. A panel can be empty for any of these and the model will
-- still say "structurally fine".
scope_covered(metric_absent).
scope_covered(label_value_absent).
scope_covered(recording_rule_broken).
scope_covered(recording_rule_deleted).
unmodelled_cause(collector_down).
unmodelled_cause(scrape_permission_denied).
unmodelled_cause(retention_expired).
unmodelled_cause(service_not_writing).
unmodelled_cause(dashboard_time_range).

-- =========================================================================
-- 1. [repo] — the dashboards
-- =========================================================================
-- A PANEL owns one or more QUERIES (Grafana calls them targets). A QUERY is
-- a list of SELECTORS: the metric-with-matchers leaves of its PromQL
-- expression. A ratio has two selectors; a plain graph has one.
--
-- The list is a cons term and not a set of facts on purpose. "Every selector
-- resolves" and "every matcher holds" are UNIVERSAL, and negation cannot
-- express them here: recording rules make the consumer graph recursive, so
-- `not broken(Q)` would put a negation inside its own cycle and the kernel
-- would reject the program. Walking a list is the positive form of "all of
-- them", exactly as examples/nope carries the assume-role chain inside the
-- fact. See README.md, "What fought back".

dashboard[repo](dash_api_overview).
dashboard[repo](dash_checkout).
dashboard[repo](dash_payments).
dashboard[repo](dash_database).
dashboard[repo](dash_canary).
dashboard[repo](dash_queues).
dashboard[repo](dash_ingest).
dashboard[repo](dash_runtime).
dashboard[repo](dash_exec_summary).
dashboard[repo](dash_capacity).

panel[repo](p_api_rps,           dash_api_overview).
panel[repo](p_api_latency,       dash_api_overview).
panel[repo](p_api_error_ratio,   dash_api_overview).
panel[repo](p_checkout_orders,   dash_checkout).
